  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Patchy valley fog this morning, especially Northwest Montana.\n \n- Warming and drying trend Friday through Sunday.\n \n- Increasing westerly winds on Monday and potential isolated \n  afternoon storms as a weak disturbance flattens the ridge. \n\n- Strong high pressure re-establishes late next week, maintaining\n  a prolonged warm and dry pattern. \n\nToday through Friday Night: Patchy morning valley fog will dissipate by\nmid- to late morning. Otherwise, zonal flow aloft will bring periodic mid-\nand high-level cloud cover through Friday, along with a few light sprinkles\nacross northwest Montana today. Winds will remain light and diurnally\ndriven through Friday morning; however, a weak disturbance tracking along\nthe Canadian border Friday afternoon will increase westerly gusts up to 25\nmph. High temperatures today will be near seasonal averages, warming to\naround 10 degrees above normal by Friday.\n\nSaturday through Next Week: An upper-level high-pressure ridge will build\noverhead this weekend, maintaining widespread sunshine and persistent warm\ndaytime temperatures. Strong radiational cooling under clear skies will\nallow valley overnight lows to drop below blended model guidance, promoting\nlocalized frost in higher valleys and typical cold spots. Monday into\nTuesday, long-range guidance depicts a weak shortwave trough sliding east\nalong the Canadian border, temporarily flattening the ridge. This system\nwill trigger an uptick in westerly winds across the Northern Rockies.\nModest afternoon destabilization may also support isolated, non-severe\nthunderstorms on Monday afternoon. Once this disturbance departs, the\nstrong upper-level ridge will rebuild across the region through mid-to-late\nweek, bringing a return to warm and dry conditions. Ensemble cluster\nguidance have trended toward a potential disturbance around \nOctober 9th-10th (~50% cluster support), which could introduce a \nbrief period of cooler temperatures, increased winds, and a \nchance of showers.",
